WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.725
Think of a pizza shop. Its rent is the same whether it makes ten pizzas or a thousand. But every extra pizza needs more cheese.

00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:17.643
Here is the pattern. Rent stays at two thousand dollars. Cheese at one dollar fifty a pizza rises as output rises.

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:30.616
Fixed costs include rent, insurance, advertising and buying a machine. Variable costs include raw materials, labour paid by output, and electricity used per hour of production.

00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:38.803
Step one. Take each cost and ask: would this change if the firm made more? Yes means variable. No means fixed.

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:51.797
Steps two and three. Keep only the group asked for. Multiply any per-hour or per-unit cost by the quantity first. Four dollars an hour for thirty hours is one hundred and twenty dollars.

00:00:52.360 --> 00:00:57.459
Step four. Add the chosen items, write the sum, and give the total with currency.

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:12.619
Here is a real question from June 2022. A clothing firm's monthly costs are rent, raw materials, insurance, and labour paid according to output. Calculate total variable costs. Two marks.

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:23.419
Rent and insurance stay the same whatever is made: fixed. Raw materials rise with output. Labour is paid according to output, so it is variable too.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:37.184
Add the two variable costs. Twelve thousand plus one hundred and fifteen thousand is one hundred and twenty-seven thousand dollars. This explanation is by olevels dot com, not an official mark scheme.

00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:44.741
A common mistake is treating labour as fixed. When pay depends on output, labour is a variable cost.

00:01:45.320 --> 00:01:50.461
Another is adding every item in the table. Add only the group the question asks for.

00:01:51.600 --> 00:02:11.717
Now you try. A furniture maker pays rent of three thousand dollars, wood of four thousand two hundred dollars, and advertising of six hundred dollars a month. A machine costs five dollars an hour to run, for one hundred and sixty hours. Calculate total fixed and total variable costs. Pause here and work it out.

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:23.677
Sort them. Rent and advertising are fixed. Wood and machine running are variable. The machine costs five times one hundred and sixty, which is eight hundred dollars.

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:30.768
Total fixed costs are three thousand six hundred dollars. Total variable costs are five thousand dollars.

00:02:31.320 --> 00:02:33.475
Here are the two totals side by side.

00:02:34.040 --> 00:02:42.552
Remember: ask if each cost changes with output, multiply any rates, then add the right group. Now try the practice questions.
